1
00:00:57,715 --> 00:01:00,025
WOMAN: Brian?

2
00:01:01,875 --> 00:01:04,343
Brian.

3
00:01:11,052 --> 00:01:14,125
David had a terrible night.
He was screaming and screaming.

4
00:01:14,126 --> 00:01:17,193
- You should have woken me.
- I couldn't sleep.

5
00:01:17,618 --> 00:01:20,451
I couldn't...

6
00:01:20,498 --> 00:01:23,649
Why don't you go back to the doctor?

7
00:01:23,698 --> 00:01:25,336
They stole my life!

8
00:01:29,138 --> 00:01:30,412
Our life.

9
00:01:33,776 --> 00:01:36,271
Come on, love, you need to sleep.

10
00:01:36,273 --> 00:01:39,590
No, that doesn't stop the headaches.

11
00:01:45,298 --> 00:01:49,530
I'll get David breakfast
and take him to the park before school.

12
00:01:52,018 --> 00:01:54,737
- I've let you both down, Lizzy.
- Oh, no.

13
00:01:54,738 --> 00:01:57,047
- I have.
- No, come on.

14
00:01:58,618 --> 00:02:01,769
It's not your fault, what they gave you.

15
00:02:05,218 --> 00:02:07,285
We can still fight this, Brian.

16
00:02:08,658 --> 00:02:10,649
Maybe it's time to give up.

17
00:02:10,698 --> 00:02:12,290
BOY: Mum! Dad!

18
00:02:12,338 --> 00:02:15,011
- I'll go.
- No, it's OK.

19
00:02:17,738 --> 00:02:19,251
Mum!

20
00:02:20,738 --> 00:02:22,968
Dad!

21
00:02:35,618 --> 00:02:38,212
All right, come on. Watch your steps.

22
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:39,771
Right. That's it.

23
00:02:39,818 --> 00:02:43,094
One, two. Good boy.

24
00:02:43,138 --> 00:02:45,333
Watch the road now.

25
00:02:45,378 --> 00:02:46,857
OK?

26
00:02:48,858 --> 00:02:51,057
We're going to go
somewhere nice, David.

27
00:02:51,058 --> 00:02:53,992
Somewhere very nice. All right?
Here you are.

28
00:04:19,498 --> 00:04:22,570
(PHONE RINGS)

29
00:04:26,378 --> 00:04:29,495
(PHONE CONTINUES RINGING)

30
00:04:33,138 --> 00:04:35,217
WOMAN: "For if the darkness
and corruption

31
00:04:35,218 --> 00:04:38,608
"leave a vestige
of the thoughts that once I had,

32
00:04:38,658 --> 00:04:42,571
"better by far
you should forget and smile

33
00:04:42,618 --> 00:04:47,009
"than that you should remember
and be sad."

34
00:04:57,338 --> 00:04:59,272
Thank you for coming, Stella.

35
00:05:08,618 --> 00:05:10,352
Well, you know where I am.

36
00:05:15,858 --> 00:05:18,659
I should have got up
with him that morning.

37
00:05:25,218 --> 00:05:27,652
Brian couldn't take it any more.

38
00:05:27,698 --> 00:05:29,211
Neither of us could.

39
00:05:30,778 --> 00:05:32,257
Not even David.

40
00:05:33,858 --> 00:05:38,329
Taking away our legal aid like they did
was the last straw.

41
00:05:39,698 --> 00:05:43,537
We might still be able to get
the drugs company into court.

42
00:05:43,538 --> 00:05:48,214
Now, I don't want to give you
any false hopes, but I won't give up.

43
00:06:08,178 --> 00:06:09,697
I won't drop this. I can't.

44
00:06:09,698 --> 00:06:12,977
Mr Justice Niven has ruled
that the decision to withdraw funding

45
00:06:12,978 --> 00:06:13,888
was reasonable.

46
00:06:13,938 --> 00:06:16,497
He's like a mouthpiece for the Government
and the drug companies.

47
00:06:16,498 --> 00:06:19,097
This soldier -
this acutely depressed soldier -

48
00:06:19,098 --> 00:06:21,937
killing himself and his child
must mean something.

49
00:06:21,938 --> 00:06:25,294
Legal aid had encouraged
them with funding,

50
00:06:25,338 --> 00:06:27,217
and the other soldiers
damaged by the vaccinations.

51
00:06:27,218 --> 00:06:29,413
Then, inches away from court,

52
00:06:29,458 --> 00:06:31,777
it was withdrawn
on instructions from the Government.

53
00:06:31,778 --> 00:06:32,817
Evidence of that?

54
00:06:32,818 --> 00:06:35,217
They know these drugs cause
neurological problems.

55
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:38,737
There's a yawning gap between
what we know and can be proven.

56
00:06:38,738 --> 00:06:41,097
John! How many more people's lives
must be ruined

57
00:06:41,098 --> 00:06:44,297
while these drug companies
make bigger and bigger profits?

58
00:06:44,298 --> 00:06:47,577
And the worst of it is that
the Army mocks and reviles

59
00:06:47,578 --> 00:06:51,337
Brian Meam and the others,
telling them they're mad and mistaken.

60
00:06:51,338 --> 00:06:53,057
Why didn't he go after the Army?

61
00:06:53,058 --> 00:06:56,337
The Army argued successfully
that the drugs weren't damaging,

62
00:06:56,338 --> 00:06:59,737
and if they were, they wouldn't
have had the means to tell, anyway.

63
00:06:59,738 --> 00:07:02,537
All Brian Meam wanted
was for someone to acknowledge

64
00:07:02,538 --> 00:07:05,057
that the cocktail of vaccinations
forced on him

65
00:07:05,058 --> 00:07:07,057
might just have caused his problems.

66
00:07:07,058 --> 00:07:08,177
And if they didn't?

67
00:07:08,178 --> 00:07:10,457
He was formally fit and healthy,
like the other soldiers were.

68
00:07:10,458 --> 00:07:13,058
His medical records clearly state that.

69
00:07:14,098 --> 00:07:16,097
What do you want me to do, Jo?

70
00:07:16,098 --> 00:07:19,010
I'm hard against the wall.

71
00:07:19,058 --> 00:07:20,977
There must be some way round this.

72
00:07:20,978 --> 00:07:24,577
The issue of legal aid being withdrawn
needs to be re-examined.

73
00:07:24,578 --> 00:07:27,377
Well, I don't see how.
Mr Justice Niven has ruled.

74
00:07:27,378 --> 00:07:31,177
John, can't you see
the injustice staring you in the face?

75
00:07:31,178 --> 00:07:34,090
Mr Justice Niven is totally biased.

76
00:07:34,138 --> 00:07:36,537
His whole family are research doctors.

77
00:07:36,538 --> 00:07:40,817
His son lives in the same village as a
lawyer from the Funding Review Committee.

78
00:07:40,818 --> 00:07:42,337
They play tennis together!

79
00:07:42,338 --> 00:07:46,331
Mr Justice Niven
is a High Court judge of long standing.

80
00:07:46,378 --> 00:07:49,617
Now, can you really
substantiate these allegations?

81
00:07:49,618 --> 00:07:52,177
At one time, you would have
stormed the citadel.

82
00:07:52,178 --> 00:07:53,293
Oh, don't start.

83
00:07:53,338 --> 00:07:57,097
Broken down the barricades.
All you needed was a whiff of injustice.

84
00:07:57,098 --> 00:07:59,232
I am sorry to have bothered you.

85
00:08:24,578 --> 00:08:27,257
- Coop!
- Oh, I wasn't expecting you back.

86
00:08:27,258 --> 00:08:30,817
See if Mr Justice Niven is still
in the building, please. I'd like a word.

87
00:08:30,818 --> 00:08:33,969
- Here, Judge, or in his room?
- Just find him.

88
00:08:35,498 --> 00:08:38,017
The decision
of the Funding Review Committee

89
00:08:38,018 --> 00:08:41,577
to withdraw the legal aid certificate
was perfectly fair, John.

90
00:08:41,578 --> 00:08:45,137
How could the Government
or anyone else have manipulated things?

91
00:08:45,138 --> 00:08:48,777
Weren't these soldiers
perfectly normal before the vaccinations?

92
00:08:48,778 --> 00:08:50,497
According to his medical record,

93
00:08:50,498 --> 00:08:53,697
there was nothing to connect
is condition causally to them.

94
00:08:53,698 --> 00:08:58,137
Counsel for the claimant suggested that
scientific evidence would soon be found -

95
00:08:58,138 --> 00:09:00,897
seems a bizarre basis
for continuing funding.

96
00:09:00,898 --> 00:09:04,817
These cases bumped along for five years,
Michael, fully funded.

97
00:09:04,818 --> 00:09:09,767
And then moments before the doors
of the High Court, funding is withdrawn.

98
00:09:09,818 --> 00:09:12,729
The tests for legal aid
are far more stringent now.

99
00:09:12,730 --> 00:09:17,097
Part 15.3 of the Funding Code says
that a certificate can be withdrawn

100
00:09:17,098 --> 00:09:20,232
when it is unreasonable
for funding to continue.

101
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:24,577
Without causal connection,
it was unreasonable for them to continue.

102
00:09:24,578 --> 00:09:29,297
15.3 also deals with the wider public
interest. Surely there was one here.

103
00:09:29,298 --> 00:09:32,574
In my view, it was addressed.

104
00:09:33,618 --> 00:09:37,685
- How did this come your way?
- I have some medical knowledge.

105
00:09:37,698 --> 00:09:38,847
Oh.

106
00:09:38,898 --> 00:09:41,457
I read medicine at Jesus
before I did law.

107
00:09:41,458 --> 00:09:45,167
Someone had to separate
law from emotion.

108
00:09:45,218 --> 00:09:48,457
There was an awful lot of emotion
surrounding these cases.

109
00:09:48,458 --> 00:09:49,697
How could there not be?

110
00:09:49,698 --> 00:09:52,577
This was just one of a number
of soldiers who committed suicide.

111
00:09:52,578 --> 00:09:57,049
It was the absence of evidence
that led me to my conclusion.

112
00:09:57,098 --> 00:10:00,698
We mustn't unreasonably damage
pharmaceutical companies

113
00:10:00,738 --> 00:10:02,377
on the basis of hysteria.

114
00:10:02,378 --> 00:10:05,857
They can't function with the prospect
of lawsuits hanging over them.

115
00:10:05,858 --> 00:10:09,137
Mistakes must sometimes be viewed
tolerantly by the courts.

116
00:10:09,138 --> 00:10:12,847
In a single case, perhaps,
but surely not on this scale?

117
00:10:12,898 --> 00:10:15,617
Nothing suggests a widespread problem.

118
00:10:15,658 --> 00:10:20,617
Pharmaceutical companies are in the front
line in the fight against bio-terrorism.

119
00:10:20,618 --> 00:10:24,377
They were helping to protect
these soldiers and everyone else.

120
00:10:24,378 --> 00:10:26,334
We mustn't impede them

121
00:10:26,378 --> 00:10:28,369
with unjustifiable actions.

122
00:10:33,258 --> 00:10:36,857
Is Mrs Mills still tied up
with that rag-bag of radicals?

123
00:10:36,858 --> 00:10:38,177
Yes.

124
00:10:38,218 --> 00:10:43,057
But that doesn't preclude the possibility
of her clients being treated unfairly.

125
00:10:43,058 --> 00:10:45,526
By Michael Niven? I doubt it.

126
00:10:45,578 --> 00:10:47,657
Oh, there is a serious risk of bias.

127
00:10:47,658 --> 00:10:50,137
His medical connections are well known.

128
00:10:50,138 --> 00:10:52,015
Doesn't his son play tennis

129
00:10:52,058 --> 00:10:54,777
with one of the lawyers
on the Funding Review Committee?

130
00:10:54,778 --> 00:10:56,737
No, no, no. That doesn't follow.

131
00:10:56,738 --> 00:10:58,697
You must accept what you are, John.

132
00:10:58,698 --> 00:11:02,008
A member of a very exclusive club.

133
00:11:16,098 --> 00:11:18,407
It can't be that bad.

134
00:11:19,458 --> 00:11:22,291
Well, I'm not suicidal. Not yet, anyway.

135
00:11:22,338 --> 00:11:25,605
Everything is improved
with another glass of wine.

136
00:11:26,618 --> 00:11:29,974
- Cheers.
- Thank you.

137
00:11:30,018 --> 00:11:33,727
(GENIAL LAUGHTER)

138
00:11:42,338 --> 00:11:44,408
We used to play this as kids.

139
00:11:44,458 --> 00:11:46,016
I know another game.

140
00:11:48,418 --> 00:11:50,773
I have a room here, Judge.

141
00:11:50,818 --> 00:11:53,286
How did you know I was a judge?

142
00:11:53,338 --> 00:11:54,817
You told me.

143
00:11:56,898 --> 00:11:58,377
Come on.

144
00:12:12,898 --> 00:12:14,331
(LIFT BELL RINGS)

145
00:12:15,858 --> 00:12:18,737
- You're very naughty.
- I haven't done anything yet.

146
00:12:18,738 --> 00:12:20,615
Yet.

147
00:12:20,658 --> 00:12:22,535
Here I am.

148
00:12:28,218 --> 00:12:31,016
Am I very drunk, Judy?

149
00:12:31,058 --> 00:12:34,297
- You're not drunk at all.
- Why is everybody staring?

150
00:12:34,298 --> 00:12:36,812
No-one is staring at you, Judge.

151
00:12:39,458 --> 00:12:44,578
Yes, I am. I am far too drunk
to be of any use to either of us.

152
00:12:44,618 --> 00:12:46,449
Thanks for the offer.

153
00:12:47,818 --> 00:12:50,457
John! Come back.

154
00:13:01,778 --> 00:13:04,617
- What happened?
- He said he was too drunk.

155
00:13:04,618 --> 00:13:06,813
OK.

156
00:13:06,858 --> 00:13:09,053
We'll catch him another time.

157
00:13:36,218 --> 00:13:38,812
(BUZZER)

158
00:13:38,858 --> 00:13:40,371
JO: Who is it?

159
00:13:40,418 --> 00:13:42,613
- Jo, it's John.
- John who?

160
00:13:42,658 --> 00:13:45,126
How many Johns do you know?

161
00:13:45,178 --> 00:13:47,294
What do you want, John?

162
00:13:47,338 --> 00:13:48,930
To talk...

163
00:13:48,978 --> 00:13:50,912
about Michael Niven's ruling.

164
00:13:58,138 --> 00:14:01,733
- MAN: Colby.
- Deed's shown up at Mrs Mills'.

165
00:14:01,778 --> 00:14:03,336
To talk about the case?

166
00:14:03,378 --> 00:14:05,414
It started that way.

167
00:14:05,458 --> 00:14:08,211
It's more about sex now. Listen.

168
00:14:09,778 --> 00:14:12,137
- JO: John. We shouldn't...
- JOHN: Shouldn't what?

169
00:14:12,138 --> 00:14:14,936
OK. So what happened? Hmm.

170
00:14:16,178 --> 00:14:21,097
All right, Tom. Now, listen. I want a full
transcript of everything said. Yeah?

171
00:14:21,098 --> 00:14:23,432
All right. Speak to you later. Bye.

172
00:14:29,818 --> 00:14:31,337
Deed is with the lawyer.

173
00:14:31,338 --> 00:14:34,257
It appears amorous,
but they are talking about the case.

174
00:14:34,258 --> 00:14:37,177
- Well, that won't benefit us, will it?
- It might, sir.

175
00:14:37,178 --> 00:14:39,457
We should go full surveillance on Deed.

176
00:14:39,458 --> 00:14:43,297
I can't procure a fully authorised tap
on a High Court judge, Major.

177
00:14:43,298 --> 00:14:46,177
That won't be necessary.
We can handle it ourselves.

178
00:14:46,178 --> 00:14:48,976
There is a lot at stake, Percy.

179
00:14:50,778 --> 00:14:52,052
Shall we?

180
00:15:05,938 --> 00:15:07,769
You gave me a turn, Judge.

181
00:15:07,818 --> 00:15:11,697
I thought perhaps someone was here
who shouldn't have been.

182
00:15:11,738 --> 00:15:13,774
- Like who?
- Oh, I don't know.

183
00:15:13,818 --> 00:15:16,697
There are weird people
creeping about from the DCA.

184
00:15:16,698 --> 00:15:20,297
There was one here this morning
who was doing an audit on equipment.

185
00:15:20,298 --> 00:15:22,687
Spying on us, more likely.

186
00:15:22,738 --> 00:15:25,072
I wondered why the door was locked.

187
00:15:25,098 --> 00:15:27,657
Did you look at
the internet plagiarism papers?

188
00:15:27,658 --> 00:15:30,809
80,000 pages?!
o, I told them I didn't want it.

189
00:15:30,858 --> 00:15:33,057
I haven't got two years to spare.

190
00:15:33,058 --> 00:15:36,417
The court manager hoped
I'd persuade you. There's no-one else.

191
00:15:36,418 --> 00:15:38,693
No. I'm not surprised. Come on!

192
00:15:38,738 --> 00:15:40,808
You looking for something?

193
00:15:40,858 --> 00:15:45,727
Yeah. Information on that soldier
who killed himself and his child.

194
00:15:45,778 --> 00:15:47,377
The papers said he was depressed.

195
00:15:47,378 --> 00:15:49,175
Yes.

196
00:15:49,218 --> 00:15:52,019
It looks like the family
went through hell.

197
00:15:55,058 --> 00:15:56,810
Judge?

198
00:15:57,898 --> 00:16:01,097
See if you can find out ow
the legal aid funding review

199
00:16:01,098 --> 00:16:03,337
went to Mr Justice Niven,
will you, please?

200
00:16:03,338 --> 00:16:06,808
- OK.
- And where Mrs Mills is this morning.

201
00:16:14,898 --> 00:16:18,137
Have you explored every avenue
on this denial of legal aid?

202
00:16:18,138 --> 00:16:20,857
John, I know you don't like
the company I keep,

203
00:16:20,858 --> 00:16:23,977
but they are competent and concerned
forjustice for the soldiers.

204
00:16:23,978 --> 00:16:26,217
Have they found you a way
round Mr Justice Niven?

205
00:16:26,218 --> 00:16:28,217
Of course not. He was far too clever.

206
00:16:28,218 --> 00:16:30,497
He's savaged the case
and threatened them with costs

207
00:16:30,498 --> 00:16:34,411
if they pressed for a full hearing
in the Divisional Court.

208
00:16:34,458 --> 00:16:35,617
Is there any new evidence?

209
00:16:35,618 --> 00:16:39,217
All routes to the full court go
through Mr Justice Niven, Sir John.

210
00:16:39,218 --> 00:16:42,297
We can't revisit the decision
to withdraw funding.

211
00:16:42,298 --> 00:16:44,857
JOHN: Unless the conduct
of the litigation was invalid.

212
00:16:44,858 --> 00:16:46,097
Are you saying it was?

213
00:16:46,098 --> 00:16:50,057
You have considered possible bias
in view of his medical connections?

214
00:16:50,058 --> 00:16:51,332
You know we have.

215
00:16:51,378 --> 00:16:54,057
Yeah. We were told it helped
is understanding of the case.

216
00:16:54,058 --> 00:16:56,017
JOHN: And what about is son's relationship

217
00:16:56,018 --> 00:16:58,777
with one of the lawyers
on the funding committee?

218
00:16:58,778 --> 00:17:01,217
That just takes the argument
back to Niven.

219
00:17:01,218 --> 00:17:05,689
Try and establish possible bias
with the proceedings before him.

220
00:17:05,738 --> 00:17:08,657
Then make an application before me
in the Divisional Court,

221
00:17:08,658 --> 00:17:10,497
if you can find anotherjudge.

222
00:17:10,498 --> 00:17:13,777
JO: Well, where are we supposed
to find one, at Woolworths?

223
00:17:13,778 --> 00:17:15,937
No. We'll get through this somehow.

224
00:17:15,938 --> 00:17:17,610
You're welcome.

225
00:17:18,938 --> 00:17:20,849
Jo!

226
00:17:20,898 --> 00:17:23,897
He's just made us
the sort of offer we can't refuse.

227
00:17:23,898 --> 00:17:27,377
There's too many complications.
Believe me, it's not worth it.

228
00:17:27,378 --> 00:17:31,312
If it gets us another day in court,
who cares how we get it?

229
00:17:32,298 --> 00:17:34,698
If I told you we had sex last night?

230
00:17:38,498 --> 00:17:41,615
Well, you're both consenting.

231
00:17:41,658 --> 00:17:44,695
Think about all those damaged soldiers.

232
00:17:44,738 --> 00:17:46,217
What about Lizzy Meam?

233
00:17:46,258 --> 00:17:50,457
She's lost her husband and her son
as a result of those vaccinations.

234
00:17:50,458 --> 00:17:53,297
And if we won and anyone
found out about me and Deed?

235
00:17:53,298 --> 00:17:55,493
Not only would we be appealed,

236
00:17:55,538 --> 00:17:58,537
we would have hefty costs against us,
and so would the soldiers.

237
00:17:58,538 --> 00:18:00,847
First, we'd have to get to court.

238
00:18:00,898 --> 00:18:04,811
Then get our funding.
Then someone would have to find out.

239
00:18:04,858 --> 00:18:06,576
Just talk to Lizzy Meam.

240
00:18:30,338 --> 00:18:33,937
Can Deed review the previous judge's
decision in this case?

241
00:18:33,938 --> 00:18:35,929
I can't see how.

242
00:18:35,978 --> 00:18:37,712
The case has been decided.

243
00:18:39,018 --> 00:18:43,534
We spent a lot of money
on intelligence, Neil, as you know.

244
00:18:43,578 --> 00:18:47,057
Then you'll know the one thing
Deed always does in his meddling

245
00:18:47,058 --> 00:18:48,925
is operate within the rules.

246
00:18:50,578 --> 00:18:55,572
The head of the DCA informs Percy Thrower
that Deed cannot reopen

247
00:18:55,618 --> 00:19:00,177
that which another High Court
judge as closed. It's unconstitutional.

248
00:19:00,178 --> 00:19:02,257
Were we to let it happen, we'd have judges
reopening anything they chose.

249
00:19:02,258 --> 00:19:06,171
And if he tries to look at
the procedure, not the merits?

250
00:19:06,218 --> 00:19:10,575
These vaccines are so very important
for our protection, Neil.

251
00:19:10,618 --> 00:19:14,377
The Government promised to ring-fence
the pharmaceutical industry

252
00:19:14,378 --> 00:19:16,857
and protect us
against compensation claims.

253
00:19:16,858 --> 00:19:20,697
We can't go on developing drugs
against bio-terrorism agents

254
00:19:20,698 --> 00:19:25,214
when our coffers are being sucked dry
by obsessive claimants.

255
00:19:25,258 --> 00:19:29,937
A development fund of 4 billion
is not likely to see the pot running dry.

256
00:19:29,938 --> 00:19:32,372
We'll deliver what we promised.

257
00:19:32,418 --> 00:19:35,457
Our Civil Contingencies Amendment Bill
will protect you

258
00:19:35,458 --> 00:19:38,097
and other major pharmaceuticals
from ever being sued.

259
00:19:38,098 --> 00:19:39,292
Good.

260
00:19:39,338 --> 00:19:42,805
It'll be law before Deed
gets off the starting block.

261
00:19:46,618 --> 00:19:49,291
I feel very bitter, Mrs Mills.

262
00:19:49,338 --> 00:19:52,887
That first lot of solicitors
we had were wicked.

263
00:19:52,938 --> 00:19:56,657
They got an expert witness
from the drug company's website!

264
00:19:56,658 --> 00:20:01,925
We didn't know any better, but they must
ave known he'd help them more than us.

265
00:20:03,858 --> 00:20:07,857
If they'd have let us get to court,
Brian and David would still be alive.

266
00:20:07,858 --> 00:20:11,258
We'd have enough money
to get proper help for Brian.

267
00:20:12,218 --> 00:20:15,737
Why do the drugs company threaten
everyone with costs like that?

268
00:20:15,738 --> 00:20:17,537
Don't they have enough money?

269
00:20:17,538 --> 00:20:20,617
Survival, profits,
that's all that matters to them.

270
00:20:20,618 --> 00:20:23,849
You somehow wish
it would happen to some of them.

271
00:20:23,898 --> 00:20:27,288
They used Brian
and all the others as guinea pigs.

272
00:20:31,458 --> 00:20:35,525
There is a chance that they could
go after us again for costs,

273
00:20:35,538 --> 00:20:37,377
if we got to court and lost.

274
00:20:37,378 --> 00:20:39,334
I don't see any point now.

275
00:20:42,538 --> 00:20:44,938
It won't bring Brian and David back.

276
00:20:50,298 --> 00:20:53,449
Maybe they've found some peace at last.

277
00:20:53,498 --> 00:20:55,632
I feel I've let you down, Lizzy.

278
00:20:59,418 --> 00:21:00,897
It's weird.

279
00:21:03,378 --> 00:21:05,175
I miss Brian's screaming.

280
00:21:07,898 --> 00:21:10,699
I'd put up with anything to have him back.

281
00:21:13,698 --> 00:21:15,417
It would start without warning

282
00:21:15,418 --> 00:21:18,952
and end up with him
banging his head against the wall.

283
00:21:20,858 --> 00:21:23,858
What made it worse
was not being able to help.

284
00:21:28,258 --> 00:21:35,175
I lie in bed and listen for sounds of him
roaming around the house unable to sleep,

285
00:21:35,218 --> 00:21:37,015
praying...

286
00:21:37,058 --> 00:21:39,811
that he and David aren't really...

287
00:21:48,858 --> 00:21:53,295
Would they get the house if we lost,
or what's not on the mortgage?

288
00:21:53,338 --> 00:21:54,817
They might try.

289
00:21:56,738 --> 00:21:58,933
Let them take it.

290
00:21:58,978 --> 00:22:01,657
I don't think I want to be here any more.

291
00:22:01,658 --> 00:22:03,977
I wouldn't want it costing you and Peter.

292
00:22:03,978 --> 00:22:06,897
I'm not sure I can pay you
ow there's no legal aid.

293
00:22:06,898 --> 00:22:08,817
Peter's agreed to do it pro bono.

294
00:22:08,818 --> 00:22:10,618
I can manage without a fee.

295
00:22:10,658 --> 00:22:15,527
Lizzy, we could shove it right back
at that pharmaceutical company,

296
00:22:15,578 --> 00:22:19,045
warn them how we might
come after them for big costs.

297
00:22:20,338 --> 00:22:22,374
If we do get this started,

298
00:22:22,418 --> 00:22:26,952
you know what'll happen if they discover
that we've recently had sex.

299
00:22:27,578 --> 00:22:29,245
JOHN: Is that all it was?

300
00:22:32,218 --> 00:22:34,737
You could get
one of the other advocates to lead.

301
00:22:34,738 --> 00:22:38,731
- You know I can't.
- You risk being disbarred.

302
00:22:38,778 --> 00:22:40,657
In the face of such injustice,
I don't see why I'd care.

303
00:22:40,658 --> 00:22:43,897
JOHN: As much as I know a lot of people
would like to get rid of me,

304
00:22:43,898 --> 00:22:45,456
I can't be disbarred.

305
00:22:47,618 --> 00:22:50,657
John, please try to get
some otherjudges to take the application.

306
00:22:50,658 --> 00:22:52,694
Who would you suggest?

307
00:22:52,738 --> 00:22:54,091
Mimi!

308
00:23:08,978 --> 00:23:12,493
The clerk in the list office
was very shirty

309
00:23:12,538 --> 00:23:15,097
about me getting
the funding review papers.

310
00:23:15,098 --> 00:23:16,937
- Did he give a reason?
- No.

311
00:23:16,938 --> 00:23:18,894
Just a load of old flannel.

312
00:23:18,938 --> 00:23:21,537
Did you find out how it went
to Michael Niven?

313
00:23:21,538 --> 00:23:23,257
Someone bypassed the list office.

314
00:23:23,258 --> 00:23:25,857
That's what put the clerk's nose
out of joint.

315
00:23:25,858 --> 00:23:27,894
Did Sir Michael ask for it?

316
00:23:27,938 --> 00:23:30,817
He was chosen
because of his medical background.

317
00:23:30,818 --> 00:23:33,537
Both his sons and his daughter
are doctors.

318
00:23:33,538 --> 00:23:35,777
And he went to college at the same time

319
00:23:35,778 --> 00:23:38,736
as Sir Ian Rochester
and Sir Percy Thrower.

320
00:23:38,778 --> 00:23:41,975
Interesting. But circumstantial.

321
00:23:42,018 --> 00:23:44,657
He certainly gave the Government
the response they wanted.

322
00:23:44,658 --> 00:23:45,693
Did he?

323
00:23:45,738 --> 00:23:49,257
Well, they're in partnership
with the drugs companies nowadays.

324
00:23:49,258 --> 00:23:53,257
Well, let's see when I approach
Ian Rochester about the case.

325
00:23:53,258 --> 00:23:54,850
That'll be fun.

326
00:23:57,818 --> 00:24:01,657
Surely it's not acceptable
to examine a brotherjudge in this way

327
00:24:01,658 --> 00:24:03,617
now the Court of Appeal's ruled?

328
00:24:03,618 --> 00:24:04,937
Not on this, it hasn't.

329
00:24:04,938 --> 00:24:07,937
Presumably it thought
the original decision was correct.

330
00:24:07,938 --> 00:24:12,257
Bias on the part of Michael Niven,
if there was any, would invalidate that.

331
00:24:12,258 --> 00:24:16,729
I don't control the list.
I can't jump a case listed for you

332
00:24:16,778 --> 00:24:18,577
in the Divisional Court
to the front of the queue.

333
00:24:18,578 --> 00:24:23,245
If a court is free, and if I can find
a judge, we can start right away.

334
00:24:26,018 --> 00:24:27,577
Aren't you supposed to be at the Hague?

335
00:24:27,578 --> 00:24:31,778
There isn't any work for me
in the International Criminal Court.

336
00:24:31,818 --> 00:24:35,174
The world's awash
with international criminals.

337
00:24:37,938 --> 00:24:41,374
Why did the original review
go to Michael Niven?

338
00:24:41,418 --> 00:24:43,857
The next most suitable judge, presumably.

339
00:24:43,858 --> 00:24:46,895
You were at Cambridge together,
weren't you?

340
00:24:46,938 --> 00:24:50,272
He was ahead of me.
I'm not sure we ever met there.

341
00:24:51,178 --> 00:24:53,931
I do need a court, Ian.

342
00:24:53,978 --> 00:24:57,617
If Mrs Cooper calls Court Services,
I'm sure they'll find you one,

343
00:24:57,618 --> 00:25:00,857
but you may be heading
into dangerous waters with this.

344
00:25:00,858 --> 00:25:02,337
Ah.

345
00:25:12,538 --> 00:25:14,893
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

346
00:25:19,738 --> 00:25:22,491
Mr Justice Niven, Judge.

347
00:25:22,538 --> 00:25:24,051
Michael.

348
00:25:24,098 --> 00:25:30,731
My clerk informs me you've asked for all
the papers in the drugs funding review.

349
00:25:30,778 --> 00:25:35,135
- I don't think we got them.
- Why do you want them?

350
00:25:35,178 --> 00:25:38,488
I've been asked to sit
in the Divisional Court.

351
00:25:38,538 --> 00:25:40,257
We're looking at your ruling

352
00:25:40,258 --> 00:25:43,977
upholding the decision
to withdraw funding from Brian Meam,

353
00:25:43,978 --> 00:25:45,696
the soldier.

354
00:25:45,738 --> 00:25:48,252
Who made this application?

355
00:25:48,298 --> 00:25:50,257
The representatives
are Radical Lawyers for Justice.

356
00:25:50,258 --> 00:25:52,818
Ah.

357
00:25:52,858 --> 00:25:55,930
The decision to withdraw was appropriate.

358
00:25:55,978 --> 00:25:57,969
There is no causal link.

359
00:25:58,018 --> 00:26:02,216
A lot of state funding
was already spent looking for a link.

360
00:26:02,258 --> 00:26:07,097
A hitherto healthy soldier was given
a cocktail of drugs for his protection

361
00:26:07,098 --> 00:26:09,373
and suffered neurological damage.

362
00:26:09,418 --> 00:26:12,417
There was a history
of ill health in his family.

363
00:26:12,418 --> 00:26:15,967
Nothing took me to the drugs as cause.

364
00:26:16,018 --> 00:26:19,897
Radical Lawyers claim
your decision is unsustainable, Michael,

365
00:26:19,898 --> 00:26:22,817
on account of your background
association with medics.

366
00:26:22,818 --> 00:26:24,410
My background...?!

367
00:26:24,458 --> 00:26:27,768
I saw no reasonable cause.

368
00:26:27,818 --> 00:26:31,457
One of your sons plays tennis
with a lawyer who sat on the committee.

369
00:26:31,458 --> 00:26:34,697
- How could that affect my judgement?!
- That's what we'll look at.

370
00:26:34,698 --> 00:26:39,032
This is not the sort of behaviour
one expects from a brotherjudge.

371
00:26:39,818 --> 00:26:41,809
We won't wear it, John.

372
00:26:45,498 --> 00:26:48,617
JO: Why would Ian Rochester lie about
being at Jesus with Michael Niven?

373
00:26:48,618 --> 00:26:51,291
The information's so easily checked.

374
00:26:51,338 --> 00:26:53,454
JOHN: They were there in '74.

375
00:26:53,498 --> 00:26:56,615
They even rowed together briefly.

376
00:26:56,658 --> 00:26:59,497
Could he be drawing you
into this for some purpose?

377
00:26:59,498 --> 00:27:01,897
- Are you getting paranoid?
- With just cause.

378
00:27:01,898 --> 00:27:05,057
Considering what's at stake for government
and pharmaceutical companies.

379
00:27:05,058 --> 00:27:06,491
Evidence?

380
00:27:06,538 --> 00:27:08,813
Intuition. Not very reliable.

381
00:27:08,858 --> 00:27:11,247
Neither is evidence sometimes.

382
00:27:11,298 --> 00:27:15,814
I've got a feeling Sir Percy Thrower
might provide a key.

383
00:27:15,858 --> 00:27:20,454
Because they were at Cambridge together?

384
00:27:20,498 --> 00:27:24,857
We keep coming back to the Home Office
and its concern with bio-terrorism, right?

385
00:27:24,858 --> 00:27:28,017
The pharmaceuticals are making
vaccines against possible attacks.

386
00:27:28,018 --> 00:27:33,490
The Army is providing them
with guinea pigs. John, it's so unjust!

387
00:27:33,538 --> 00:27:37,577
They did that with soldiers in the '50s,
testing the effects of nuclear weapons.

388
00:27:37,578 --> 00:27:39,091
One fight at a time.

389
00:27:41,618 --> 00:27:45,372
I'd concentrate on
Michael Niven's possible bias.

390
00:27:46,938 --> 00:27:48,530
Do you want to eat?

391
00:27:48,578 --> 00:27:50,933
JO: Not in the circumstances.

392
00:27:50,978 --> 00:27:53,897
Circumstantially,
eating's no different from having a drink.

393
00:27:53,898 --> 00:27:56,765
- It's what happens afterwards.
- Excuse me.

394
00:27:59,698 --> 00:28:01,768
- How are you?
- Have we met?

395
00:28:01,818 --> 00:28:03,774
Ian Rochester's party.

396
00:28:03,818 --> 00:28:05,331
Wrong party. Sorry.

397
00:28:13,578 --> 00:28:16,817
You really don't let the grass
grow under your feet, do you?

398
00:28:16,818 --> 00:28:18,376
No.

399
00:28:22,218 --> 00:28:24,049
What's all this?

400
00:28:24,098 --> 00:28:28,011
Papers from the Judicial Review
Mr Justice Niven conducted.

401
00:28:28,058 --> 00:28:30,897
I'm looking at why the soldier's funding
was withdrawn,

402
00:28:30,898 --> 00:28:33,017
not writing a history of legal aid.

403
00:28:33,018 --> 00:28:34,857
This is just a quarter of it!

404
00:28:34,858 --> 00:28:37,937
That's one way of slowing us up.
Where's my policeman?

405
00:28:37,938 --> 00:28:42,539
- You banished him to the library.
- I've got something for him to do.

406
00:28:43,138 --> 00:28:44,297
Have you taken up smoking?

407
00:28:44,298 --> 00:28:46,097
I want him to check
that for fingerprints.

408
00:28:46,098 --> 00:28:47,857
I think the owner might be following me.

409
00:28:47,858 --> 00:28:49,735
- Why?
- Don't tell Stephen.

410
00:28:49,778 --> 00:28:53,257
- He'll smother me with protection.
- But if they're following you?

411
00:28:53,258 --> 00:28:56,257
Well, if we find out who,
we might find out why.

412
00:28:56,258 --> 00:28:59,011
Tell him it's urgent. Oh, Coop.

413
00:28:59,058 --> 00:29:02,130
No more papers.
I'll ask questions instead.

414
00:29:02,178 --> 00:29:03,657
Yes.

415
00:29:05,338 --> 00:29:07,568
Is Deed getting this case on?

416
00:29:07,618 --> 00:29:11,167
I wish I could say he wasn't, Percy.

417
00:29:11,218 --> 00:29:14,255
Caversham are feeling persecuted by him.

418
00:29:14,298 --> 00:29:18,977
He's looking into the possibility of
apparent bias on Mr Justice Niven's part.

419
00:29:18,978 --> 00:29:20,172
A clever move.

420
00:29:20,218 --> 00:29:25,685
It means he isn't seen to be trespassing
on the substance of the litigation itself.

421
00:29:28,978 --> 00:29:33,688
If the soldier's widow
gets funding reinstated,

422
00:29:33,738 --> 00:29:38,607
she'd pursue the pharmaceutical
companies through the courts.

423
00:29:38,658 --> 00:29:40,857
Some of our best lawyers are looking into

424
00:29:40,858 --> 00:29:43,657
why Deed shouldn't re-examine
the Niven ruling.

425
00:29:43,658 --> 00:29:46,616
And if he does, Ian?

426
00:29:46,658 --> 00:29:48,897
Does it matter if this soldier's widow
gets to court?

427
00:29:48,898 --> 00:29:50,417
Perhaps we owe her the truth.

428
00:29:50,418 --> 00:29:53,897
- The fallout could be huge.
- Caversham might up and quit Britain.

429
00:29:53,898 --> 00:29:55,217
It's more than lost jobs.

430
00:29:55,218 --> 00:29:58,577
They are the vanguard in the PM's
fight against bio-terrorism.

431
00:29:58,578 --> 00:30:02,577
We could speed up the Civil Contingencies
Amendment Bill, Home Secretary.

432
00:30:02,578 --> 00:30:03,937
Give them the protection they need.

433
00:30:03,938 --> 00:30:07,055
Too much haste
and we might be spotted.

434
00:30:09,898 --> 00:30:14,176
Efforts are being made
to hinder Deed, Home Secretary.

435
00:30:14,218 --> 00:30:16,697
For once, Percy,
I'd like to stop him legally.

436
00:30:16,698 --> 00:30:19,632
That would really take him down
a peg or two.

437
00:30:21,578 --> 00:30:24,092
Judge...

438
00:30:25,498 --> 00:30:30,333
...the court manager says
there's no court available.

439
00:30:30,378 --> 00:30:33,848
I do know that 5 and 27 are free.

440
00:30:33,898 --> 00:30:36,537
- OK, grab one of those.
- When are you sitting?

441
00:30:36,538 --> 00:30:38,377
As soon as I can find anotherjudge.

442
00:30:38,378 --> 00:30:40,017
I'll warn the other parties.

443
00:30:40,018 --> 00:30:41,497
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

444
00:30:43,378 --> 00:30:45,097
Is the judge in, Mrs Cooper?

445
00:30:45,098 --> 00:30:48,937
Ah, come in, Stephen. Did you get
a result on those fingerprints?

446
00:30:48,938 --> 00:30:52,097
Well, I did get a response.
I can't have the information.

447
00:30:52,098 --> 00:30:54,297
- What's that mean?
- I don't have enough authority.

448
00:30:54,298 --> 00:30:56,217
Are there fingerprints on record?

449
00:30:56,218 --> 00:30:58,097
There are, but I can't find out whose.

450
00:30:58,098 --> 00:31:01,217
They could belong
to a special informant, Judge,

451
00:31:01,218 --> 00:31:03,137
or a member of the security services.

452
00:31:03,138 --> 00:31:04,207
Ah.

453
00:31:04,258 --> 00:31:07,534
Who would have the authority to tell me?

454
00:31:07,578 --> 00:31:08,912
I don't know, Judge.

455
00:31:08,938 --> 00:31:11,611
Perhaps my boss, Superintendent Gaghan.

456
00:31:11,658 --> 00:31:13,091
OK. Bring him here.

457
00:31:13,138 --> 00:31:15,174
Her. She's a her.

458
00:31:22,178 --> 00:31:23,657
The court, Coop.

459
00:31:29,938 --> 00:31:32,897
We wouldn't presume to withhold
information from you.

460
00:31:32,898 --> 00:31:34,937
Whose prints were on the cigarettes?

461
00:31:34,938 --> 00:31:37,817
The unidentified prints,
we assumed were yours.

462
00:31:37,818 --> 00:31:39,137
The smudged prints, we couldn't identify.

463
00:31:39,138 --> 00:31:40,897
Then whose record
was denied to PC Ashurst?

464
00:31:40,898 --> 00:31:43,257
They didn't relate to those
on the cigarette packet, sir.

465
00:31:43,258 --> 00:31:45,817
How do you know
if you didn't identify them?

466
00:31:45,818 --> 00:31:47,774
It was a mistake, Sir John.

467
00:31:47,818 --> 00:31:51,817
Do you know what the penalties are
for lying to a High Court judge?

468
00:31:51,818 --> 00:31:54,252
No-one's lying to you, sir.

469
00:31:54,298 --> 00:31:56,497
I asked if you knew
what the penalties were.

470
00:31:56,498 --> 00:31:57,498
Yes, sir.

471
00:31:57,538 --> 00:32:01,217
If I find out that I'm being deceived,
that person will go to prison.

472
00:32:01,218 --> 00:32:04,537
And I don't care if it's
the Commissioner of Police himself.

473
00:32:04,538 --> 00:32:06,737
- Do I make myself clear?
- Yes, Sir John.

474
00:32:06,738 --> 00:32:11,209
Yes. Good. Well, I want that cigarette
packet returned to my office.

475
00:32:21,098 --> 00:32:23,432
He wants the cigarette packet back.

476
00:32:57,658 --> 00:32:59,888
You didn't go up in the world.

477
00:32:59,938 --> 00:33:02,498
The quality of my client has gone up.

478
00:33:04,178 --> 00:33:05,452
How so?

479
00:33:05,498 --> 00:33:08,965
All a lot more deserving
than rapists and embezzlers.

480
00:33:10,738 --> 00:33:13,777
You gave up the chance
of being a judge for this?

481
00:33:13,778 --> 00:33:16,292
- Yeah, I've no regrets.
- Good.

482
00:33:17,858 --> 00:33:19,857
We're planning on moving out of here.

483
00:33:19,858 --> 00:33:21,814
Where to? A sub-basement?

484
00:33:21,858 --> 00:33:24,417
We're going to make our chambers virtual.

485
00:33:24,418 --> 00:33:26,817
That way we can spend
more money on the client.

486
00:33:26,818 --> 00:33:28,417
Where are you going to meet them,
in the public library?

487
00:33:28,418 --> 00:33:31,330
Most of my clients tend to be in custody.

488
00:33:31,378 --> 00:33:32,417
Lizzy Meam isn't.

489
00:33:32,418 --> 00:33:36,217
If you win that one, you'll have legions
beating a path to your door.

490
00:33:36,218 --> 00:33:37,857
No point being virtual then.

491
00:33:37,858 --> 00:33:39,897
Yeah. Let's get to the court first.

492
00:33:39,898 --> 00:33:44,737
Have you got a very good expert witness?
Because you're going to need one.

493
00:33:44,738 --> 00:33:46,456
Dr Peter Hiaasen.

494
00:33:46,498 --> 00:33:48,977
He's a leading gastroenterologist
in Miami.

495
00:33:48,978 --> 00:33:52,017
JOHN: I thought these soldiers'problems
were neurological.

496
00:33:52,018 --> 00:33:54,657
JO: His thesis is
that the problems start in the stomach.

497
00:33:54,658 --> 00:33:59,778
- Can you afford to bring him over?
- He's here already doing research.

498
00:33:59,818 --> 00:34:03,128
Is there a possibility
that you might be followed?

499
00:34:03,178 --> 00:34:06,568
- What, stalked?
- I'm serious.

500
00:34:06,618 --> 00:34:08,097
Why, what's happened?

501
00:34:10,418 --> 00:34:13,977
I want to move quickly.
Can you put your people on notice?

502
00:34:13,978 --> 00:34:15,778
Coop's already notified us.

503
00:34:17,978 --> 00:34:20,497
John,
we really do ave to keep our distance.

504
00:34:20,498 --> 00:34:22,329
It's a bit late for that!

505
00:34:22,378 --> 00:34:25,112
JOHN: Yeah. I'm on my way
to meet Charlie.

506
00:34:28,418 --> 00:34:29,851
CHARLIE: I miss Jo.

507
00:34:29,898 --> 00:34:32,537
Everything in chambers
seems somehow different now.

508
00:34:32,538 --> 00:34:34,017
How?

509
00:34:34,058 --> 00:34:36,937
I don't know.
Everything's about the bottom line.

510
00:34:36,938 --> 00:34:40,097
- You're not going to join her?
- I don't have any work.

511
00:34:40,098 --> 00:34:41,937
Why? You're a good advocate.

512
00:34:41,938 --> 00:34:44,057
There are a lot of good advocates, Dad.

513
00:34:44,058 --> 00:34:45,777
In case you hadn't noticed,

514
00:34:45,778 --> 00:34:49,217
the Legal Services Commission
are cutting back on criminal defence.

515
00:34:49,218 --> 00:34:52,257
The Government doesn't value
an independent bar.

516
00:34:52,258 --> 00:34:56,809
They want a public defender system
run along the lines of the CPS.

517
00:34:56,858 --> 00:34:58,576
I might have to sign up.

518
00:35:00,138 --> 00:35:02,377
Are you going to do this work for me?

519
00:35:02,378 --> 00:35:05,857
A medical researcher
would do the job faster and better.

520
00:35:05,858 --> 00:35:08,977
Your legal mind will appreciate
what's admissible.

521
00:35:08,978 --> 00:35:12,175
- How urgent is it?
- All I need is a court.

522
00:35:12,218 --> 00:35:14,417
Well, you'd better feed me first.

523
00:35:14,418 --> 00:35:16,737
Why else would I come all this way?

524
00:35:16,738 --> 00:35:18,217
I'm hungry.

525
00:35:22,698 --> 00:35:24,177
(ENGINE STARTS)

526
00:35:26,978 --> 00:35:30,288
Hmm, hmm, yep, yep.

527
00:35:30,338 --> 00:35:32,806
Yes, you can leave that with me.

528
00:35:32,858 --> 00:35:35,213
Thank you. Bye.

529
00:35:35,258 --> 00:35:38,497
You were a lot more polite
in Sussex, Mr Harrison.

530
00:35:38,498 --> 00:35:40,165
I do apologise, Sir John.

531
00:35:40,178 --> 00:35:41,817
This court can't be used, sir.

532
00:35:41,818 --> 00:35:44,577
- Where was the asbestos found?
- In the roof, Sir John.

533
00:35:44,578 --> 00:35:46,011
When was it found?

534
00:35:46,058 --> 00:35:48,057
Quite recently.
When a leak was being repaired.

535
00:35:48,058 --> 00:35:50,017
It's been there for God knows how long.

536
00:35:50,018 --> 00:35:51,857
Another day won't make any difference.

537
00:35:51,858 --> 00:35:55,017
I'm afraid health and safety
won't permit that, Sir John.

538
00:35:55,018 --> 00:35:56,737
We'll use Court Number Five, Coop.

539
00:35:56,738 --> 00:35:58,657
- Yes, Judge.
- Not free, Sir John.

540
00:35:58,658 --> 00:36:01,570
Mr Justice Niven
is hearing an appeal there.

541
00:36:01,618 --> 00:36:04,218
- Since when?
- It started this morning.

542
00:36:05,578 --> 00:36:07,694
Find me another court.

543
00:36:11,738 --> 00:36:15,731
Is this what you did for promotion, Brian?
What's going on?

544
00:36:15,778 --> 00:36:17,848
Nothing, Rita.

545
00:36:17,898 --> 00:36:23,018
Rita, could you get him
to hear the internet copyright case?

546
00:36:26,058 --> 00:36:30,057
Judge, there is a court free
for the internet copyright case.

547
00:36:30,058 --> 00:36:31,817
Now, you could take that case...

548
00:36:31,818 --> 00:36:33,777
Two or more years of hell?
o, thank you.

549
00:36:33,778 --> 00:36:38,017
Yes, but you could open, order
an adjournment for a technical reason,

550
00:36:38,018 --> 00:36:40,691
and have the funding review on standby.

551
00:36:40,738 --> 00:36:43,257
And afterwards, I'd still have
the mind-numbing internet case.

552
00:36:43,258 --> 00:36:45,057
I'm sure you could get out of it, Judge.

553
00:36:45,058 --> 00:36:47,572
- Thanks, Coop.
- Thank Mr Harrison.

554
00:37:29,338 --> 00:37:31,249
Yes, Dr Mary Moon, please.

555
00:37:31,298 --> 00:37:33,337
OPERATOR: Do you have an address?

556
00:37:33,338 --> 00:37:38,366
Try Chiddingley, East Sussex.
Sorry, I don't have a street.

557
00:37:38,418 --> 00:37:42,536
Sorry, the only one listed
is ex-directory.

558
00:37:42,578 --> 00:37:44,534
OK. Thanks.

559
00:37:49,538 --> 00:37:54,658
- (PHONE RINGS)
- JOHN: Charlie.

560
00:37:54,698 --> 00:37:56,734
Oh, Dad.

561
00:37:56,778 --> 00:38:00,457
Well, you could at least try and sound
a little less disappointed.

562
00:38:00,458 --> 00:38:02,417
I thought it was Tom Cruise
returning my call.

563
00:38:02,418 --> 00:38:03,851
Tom who?

564
00:38:03,898 --> 00:38:07,527
- Dad, he's a Hollywood movie star.
- Charlie!

565
00:38:07,578 --> 00:38:11,417
I'm on all these support sites
for soldiers damaged by vaccinations.

566
00:38:11,418 --> 00:38:12,897
It's really interesting stuff.

567
00:38:12,898 --> 00:38:15,935
Yeah. I read some of them. A bit loony.

568
00:38:15,978 --> 00:38:19,334
- Do you know who Dr Mary Moon is?
- Should I?

569
00:38:19,378 --> 00:38:22,337
She and Dr Rupert Dayvis
caused a big scandal last year

570
00:38:22,338 --> 00:38:26,097
when they went on record saying
the cocktail of drugs soldiers are given

571
00:38:26,098 --> 00:38:29,017
is causing all their mental
and emotional problems.

572
00:38:29,018 --> 00:38:33,577
Dr Dayvis killed himself by tying a rope
around a tree and then his neck

573
00:38:33,578 --> 00:38:35,177
and then driving away in his car.

574
00:38:35,178 --> 00:38:39,777
Oh, yes. Didn't she accuse the
pharmaceutical companies of killing him?

575
00:38:39,778 --> 00:38:42,297
CHARLIE: Along with all the others
with vested interests.

576
00:38:42,298 --> 00:38:46,737
The internet sites insist that he was
killed because of what they discovered.

577
00:38:46,738 --> 00:38:49,697
- So why wasn't Dr Moon killed as well?
- Don't know.

578
00:38:49,698 --> 00:38:51,457
CHARLIE: I'll ask her if I can locate her.

579
00:38:51,458 --> 00:38:54,217
Could I get her details
through the court people?

580
00:38:54,218 --> 00:38:57,137
JOHN: Well, you could ask Coop to try,
but don't tell me about it.

581
00:38:57,138 --> 00:39:00,733
Charlie, be careful around this,
won't you?

582
00:39:00,778 --> 00:39:02,052
Dad?

583
00:39:02,098 --> 00:39:05,937
Well, there's a lot at stake for those
companies. The government as well.

584
00:39:05,938 --> 00:39:07,817
Isn't that being a bit paranoid?

585
00:39:07,818 --> 00:39:10,337
Is that why I can't find anotherjudge
to sit with me?

586
00:39:10,338 --> 00:39:12,249
Have you asked Grandad?

587
00:39:12,298 --> 00:39:14,417
I don't think that's very likely.

588
00:39:14,418 --> 00:39:18,177
CHARLIE: Hey. In the words of the song,
"The man in the gabardine mac is a spy."

589
00:39:18,178 --> 00:39:20,977
I don't get paid to listen to that.
That is off!

590
00:39:20,978 --> 00:39:24,645
JOHN: It's the man in the gabardine suit
that's the spy.

591
00:39:32,058 --> 00:39:37,690
Here we are. The summons office
got her details from the police.

592
00:39:37,738 --> 00:39:41,097
CHARLIE: I'll remember that
when I'm trying to locate a witness.

593
00:39:41,098 --> 00:39:45,177
COOPER: Charlie, if your dad ever gets
anotherjudge to hear this with him,

594
00:39:45,178 --> 00:39:47,312
you didn't get that from me, OK?

595
00:39:48,458 --> 00:39:52,657
With a bit more notice, my darling,
I would have offered you coffee.

596
00:39:52,658 --> 00:39:55,297
But you see, I'm sitting.

597
00:39:55,338 --> 00:39:58,171
Why can't you help Dad with this case?

598
00:39:58,218 --> 00:40:00,095
Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

599
00:40:00,138 --> 00:40:06,407
I've known Michael Niven
for a very long time.

600
00:40:06,458 --> 00:40:09,052
He's a decent fellow.

601
00:40:09,098 --> 00:40:13,257
But what if he's an unsuitable judge
for reasons of apparent bias?

602
00:40:13,258 --> 00:40:16,614
Like in Taylor and Laurence, 2003?

603
00:40:16,658 --> 00:40:19,218
(HE LAUGHS)

604
00:40:19,258 --> 00:40:21,852
That's rather good, clever girl.

605
00:40:21,898 --> 00:40:23,456
Yes.

606
00:40:59,178 --> 00:41:00,978
Lizzy, is this a good idea?

607
00:41:15,098 --> 00:41:16,577
(BANGING ON DOOR)

608
00:41:16,618 --> 00:41:18,097
All rise.

609
00:41:30,738 --> 00:41:33,377
This threatens to be
a very long-running case.

610
00:41:33,378 --> 00:41:37,737
Mr McCartney is a litigant in person
and may not be familiar with procedure.

611
00:41:37,738 --> 00:41:40,657
I have done a lot of research
on the internet, my lord.

612
00:41:40,658 --> 00:41:43,125
Yes. I'm sure you have, Mr McCartney.

613
00:41:43,138 --> 00:41:49,407
There are 87,000 pages on the internet
relating to this case.

614
00:41:49,458 --> 00:41:51,528
Now, I've calculated...

615
00:41:51,578 --> 00:41:55,017
that if I read a page a minute
for eight hours a day, every day,

616
00:41:55,018 --> 00:41:57,486
I'd still need over half a year.

617
00:41:57,538 --> 00:41:59,130
That isn't to say

618
00:41:59,178 --> 00:42:04,411
that we won't deal diligently
with the realities of this case.

619
00:42:04,458 --> 00:42:09,134
Ideas that Mr McCartney posted
on the internet were purportedly

620
00:42:09,178 --> 00:42:14,298
exploited for commercial gain
by Internet Raiders Incorporated.

621
00:42:14,338 --> 00:42:16,408
So, two questions arise.

622
00:42:16,458 --> 00:42:22,135
Did Mr McCartney's ideas constitute
something that could be legally owned

623
00:42:22,178 --> 00:42:24,772
and with copyright vested in him?

624
00:42:24,818 --> 00:42:28,094
And, if so,
did Internet Raiders Incorporated

625
00:42:28,138 --> 00:42:32,370
wrongly appropriate those ideas
for commercial gain?

626
00:42:32,418 --> 00:42:38,050
- McCARTNEY: They did, sir.
- That's what we're here to discover.

627
00:42:38,098 --> 00:42:39,326
Mr Norwalk?

628
00:42:39,378 --> 00:42:44,247
Might I enquire about the application
we made under rule 34.13

629
00:42:44,298 --> 00:42:48,817
for the court to issue a letter of request
for an absent witness deposition?

630
00:42:48,818 --> 00:42:52,970
The request was made
to the Supreme Court in Buenos Aires.

631
00:42:53,018 --> 00:42:56,817
Our witness, Mr Kray, is critical
to the structure of our case.

632
00:42:56,818 --> 00:43:01,897
The court in Argentina has not managed to
track your intended deponent, Mr Norwalk.

633
00:43:01,898 --> 00:43:04,048
Ah.

634
00:43:04,098 --> 00:43:07,657
Mr McCartney, do you intend to make
an opening address to the court?

635
00:43:07,658 --> 00:43:10,491
I believe that's allowed, sir.

636
00:43:10,538 --> 00:43:13,848
Yes, it is. I would just
ask you to keep it short.

637
00:43:21,138 --> 00:43:22,617
(DOORBELL RINGS)

638
00:43:53,578 --> 00:43:55,534
Dr Moon?

639
00:43:55,578 --> 00:43:59,412
- Do I know you?
- No. Well, I'm doing some research.

640
00:43:59,458 --> 00:44:02,217
- Mr Justice Deed is hearing a case...
- I can't help you. I'm sorry.

641
00:44:02,218 --> 00:44:04,377
You and Dr Dayvis
discovered a connection

642
00:44:04,378 --> 00:44:07,417
between the drugs
and neurological damage to the soldiers.

643
00:44:07,418 --> 00:44:11,497
There is no proof the drugs damaged them.
You shouldn't be looking at this.

644
00:44:11,498 --> 00:44:13,614
That wasn't Dr Dayvis's view.

645
00:44:13,658 --> 00:44:16,658
- What is it you want?
- Access to his papers.

646
00:44:17,898 --> 00:44:19,809
I can't talk. Go away.

647
00:44:19,858 --> 00:44:22,737
Well, if you change your mind,
ere's my number.

648
00:44:22,738 --> 00:44:26,737
The judge's number is on the back.
He will take this to court.

649
00:44:26,738 --> 00:44:29,047
He is wasting his time.

650
00:44:29,098 --> 00:44:31,417
JOHN: Are you saying
someone's threatened her?

651
00:44:31,418 --> 00:44:34,617
CHARLIE: Their discovery did cause
a lot of fuss in medical circles.

652
00:44:34,618 --> 00:44:37,777
If they're right, the soldiers' cases
would be watertight.

653
00:44:37,778 --> 00:44:40,975
The internet sites claim
Dr Dayvis held the key.

654
00:44:41,018 --> 00:44:46,251
He and Dr Moon identified
the specific damage as neural deficit.

655
00:44:46,298 --> 00:44:51,418
Some sites scrupulously tracked
the rise in neural deficit

656
00:44:51,458 --> 00:44:55,297
and bowel disorders in soldiers
following their revelations.

657
00:44:55,298 --> 00:44:57,129
What is neural deficit?

658
00:44:57,178 --> 00:45:02,127
Well, it's the... It's the permanent
breaks in neural pathways.

659
00:45:02,178 --> 00:45:04,937
They carry information
to all parts of the body.

660
00:45:04,938 --> 00:45:08,737
Any interruption stops
proper functioning of all organisms.

661
00:45:08,738 --> 00:45:13,368
What's not known is why some parts
are affected and others not.

662
00:45:13,418 --> 00:45:17,855
Inflammation of spinal nerve cells
causes terrible pain.

663
00:45:17,898 --> 00:45:20,232
Sufferers scream, bang their heads.

664
00:45:20,258 --> 00:45:23,295
On their best days,
they're just depressed.

665
00:45:23,338 --> 00:45:26,697
Other vaccinated soldiers
ave killed themselves too, Dad.

666
00:45:26,698 --> 00:45:29,897
JOHN: And is there a background
prevalence to these health problems?

667
00:45:29,898 --> 00:45:31,457
CHARLIE: There's bound to be,

668
00:45:31,458 --> 00:45:34,537
but nothing's shown up prior to
the soldiers getting the vaccinations,

669
00:45:34,538 --> 00:45:37,848
which were arguably experimental.
Why is that?

670
00:45:37,898 --> 00:45:41,057
You're thinking like counsel
for the claimant, Charlie.

671
00:45:41,058 --> 00:45:44,977
Look, Dad, you don't need to show
that these drugs are causing the problem,

672
00:45:44,978 --> 00:45:47,657
just that there is
a significant possibility.

673
00:45:47,658 --> 00:45:52,217
Well, I'm supposed to be concerned with
the procedure in the Niven hearing.

674
00:45:52,218 --> 00:45:57,417
The merits and whether Dr Moon is too
scared to help should be for another day.

675
00:45:57,418 --> 00:46:01,934
Dad, the merits were strong
until Caversham got at all the experts.

676
00:46:01,978 --> 00:46:05,057
If you're looking for bias,
how bent is all this?

677
00:46:05,058 --> 00:46:09,131
And they would say
"irrational conspiracy theory".

678
00:46:09,178 --> 00:46:11,294
No, you keep looking, Charlie.

679
00:46:11,338 --> 00:46:14,774
McCARTHY: The idea behind
the World Wide Web,

680
00:46:14,818 --> 00:46:18,697
perhaps the most consequential
known to mankind,

681
00:46:18,738 --> 00:46:22,208
was the instant dissemination
of information

682
00:46:22,258 --> 00:46:27,093
in order to help free people
of all colours and creeds,

683
00:46:27,138 --> 00:46:29,971
not the theft of ideas.

684
00:46:30,018 --> 00:46:32,293
Quite the opposite, in fact.

685
00:46:32,338 --> 00:46:36,047
Ideas are currency in this technology,

686
00:46:36,098 --> 00:46:40,774
and the fact that they can go
around the world and back in an instant

687
00:46:40,818 --> 00:46:43,491
makes them more liable to theft.

688
00:46:46,538 --> 00:46:47,732
Excuse me.

689
00:46:47,778 --> 00:46:55,207
Did Dr Alexander Graham Bell have the idea
for the phone first, or Marconi?

690
00:46:55,258 --> 00:47:00,651
It's unlikely that they both had
the same idea at exactly the same time.

691
00:47:00,698 --> 00:47:04,532
Things simply moved more slowly
in those days

692
00:47:04,578 --> 00:47:10,733
and it only looked like they had
the idea at the exact same time.

693
00:47:10,778 --> 00:47:15,137
Now, the expressed intention
of the inventor of the World Wide Web,

694
00:47:15,138 --> 00:47:20,610
Sir Tim Berners-Lee,
was to free up ideas and people.

695
00:47:20,658 --> 00:47:24,367
He applied for no royalties or patents.

696
00:47:24,418 --> 00:47:28,206
Let me take you back to 1969
in America...

697
00:47:28,258 --> 00:47:31,967
Mr McCartney, is it your intention
to set the record

698
00:47:32,018 --> 00:47:35,169
for the longest opening in a civil action?

699
00:47:35,218 --> 00:47:38,369
The record so far is 119 days.

700
00:47:39,378 --> 00:47:43,817
I'm just trying to set out the facts
and context as clearly as I can.

701
00:47:43,818 --> 00:47:46,207
Yes, well, sometimes less is more.

702
00:47:46,258 --> 00:47:49,392
If we'd had a jury,
you'd have lost them by now.

703
00:47:49,698 --> 00:47:51,177
Do you want me to stop?

704
00:47:51,178 --> 00:47:54,488
Not if you think
I don't have the point.

705
00:47:54,538 --> 00:47:58,326
Now, what I'm going to do
is adjourn now.

706
00:47:58,378 --> 00:48:03,930
Think overnight about how much more
you want me to know.

707
00:48:03,978 --> 00:48:05,457
All rise.

708
00:48:18,818 --> 00:48:21,577
Coop tells me you've got
a problem with your client.

709
00:48:21,578 --> 00:48:23,897
- Yeah, she's cracking up.
- Is she ready to go?

710
00:48:23,898 --> 00:48:26,651
Well, if you've got anotherjudge.

711
00:48:26,698 --> 00:48:28,575
We'll get there.

712
00:48:28,618 --> 00:48:31,655
John, is this going to happen?

713
00:48:31,698 --> 00:48:35,257
Coop, warn the other parties
in the Funding Review to stand by.

714
00:48:35,258 --> 00:48:37,577
COOPER: Will I tell the Internet Raiders
to stand down?

715
00:48:37,578 --> 00:48:40,978
JOHN: If we do, we'll alert
the world and his uncle.

716
00:49:04,738 --> 00:49:06,694
(DOOR OPENS)

717
00:49:22,898 --> 00:49:24,032
(CHARLIE SCREAMS)

718
00:49:44,658 --> 00:49:47,297
Charlie ought to move
from this ghastly area.

719
00:49:47,298 --> 00:49:49,417
There's nothing wrong with the area,
George.

720
00:49:49,418 --> 00:49:51,215
Then why was she attacked?

721
00:49:53,258 --> 00:49:54,896
(KNOCK ON DOOR)

722
00:49:56,618 --> 00:49:59,530
Oh, Charlie!

723
00:49:59,578 --> 00:50:01,489
What happened?

724
00:50:01,538 --> 00:50:02,857
Were you hurt, darling?

725
00:50:02,858 --> 00:50:06,697
I don't know what's more shocking,
this or seeing my parents together.

726
00:50:06,698 --> 00:50:08,777
It looks like an opportunistic burglary.

727
00:50:08,778 --> 00:50:11,137
Looks like someone's got a grudge
against my daughter.

728
00:50:11,138 --> 00:50:14,177
It was probably Tom Cruise,
because I stood him up.

729
00:50:14,178 --> 00:50:16,777
- Is that a boyfriend, miss?
- I think it was a joke.

730
00:50:16,778 --> 00:50:18,769
I certainly hope so.

731
00:50:18,818 --> 00:50:21,048
Oh, yeah, Tom Cruise.

732
00:50:21,098 --> 00:50:26,331
- Did they get anything?
- Hard to say. Not my TV or my iPod.

733
00:50:26,378 --> 00:50:27,737
They must have missed them.

734
00:50:27,738 --> 00:50:31,333
They took my laptop
with all the research.

735
00:50:31,378 --> 00:50:33,977
- Did you see them, Chas?
- Only a blur, I'm afraid.

736
00:50:33,978 --> 00:50:36,970
It's probably as well, darling.

737
00:50:37,018 --> 00:50:39,457
Well, you can't stay here.
Come home with me.

738
00:50:39,458 --> 00:50:42,977
Yeah, the Home Secretary can provide
round-the-clock protection.

739
00:50:42,978 --> 00:50:45,057
- His name is Neil.
- Yeah, I know his name.

740
00:50:45,058 --> 00:50:48,377
Would you be able to come to the station
some time and sign a statement, Charlie?

741
00:50:48,378 --> 00:50:51,497
No, no, no. Get the CID onto this.
Check for fingerprints.

742
00:50:51,498 --> 00:50:53,170
It's just a burglary.

743
00:50:55,058 --> 00:50:57,253
Yes, Sir John. Of course.

744
00:50:57,298 --> 00:51:00,097
Why don't you call
Supt Gaghan at Scotland Yard?

745
00:51:00,098 --> 00:51:02,165
I'm sure she's bound to help(!)

746
00:51:05,178 --> 00:51:07,134
Who's Supt Gaghan, Dad?

747
00:51:07,178 --> 00:51:09,657
Someone who's as useless
as the Home Secretary.

748
00:51:09,658 --> 00:51:11,577
- Don't listen to him.
- Any comment?

749
00:51:11,578 --> 00:51:15,337
- It's a minor break-in.
- Is law and order getting out of control?

750
00:51:15,338 --> 00:51:17,217
If that's the line you're running.

751
00:51:17,218 --> 00:51:19,737
There have been more
drug-related robberies.

752
00:51:19,738 --> 00:51:21,497
Best talk to the Home Secretary
about that.

753
00:51:21,498 --> 00:51:23,697
- What's going on, Dad?
- I don't know, Chas.

754
00:51:23,698 --> 00:51:25,697
Are Caversham coming after us?

755
00:51:25,698 --> 00:51:27,537
They're the ones
with the most to lose.

756
00:51:27,538 --> 00:51:28,977
It could have been anyone.

757
00:51:28,978 --> 00:51:33,057
There were two of them. They
didn't take er iPod, or even her money.

758
00:51:33,058 --> 00:51:34,337
They could've missed them.

759
00:51:34,338 --> 00:51:36,454
Burglars don't miss a sixpence.

760
00:51:36,498 --> 00:51:38,136
What was in your laptop?

761
00:51:38,178 --> 00:51:39,896
The usual stuff.

762
00:51:39,938 --> 00:51:41,538
Notes on neural deficit,

763
00:51:41,578 --> 00:51:45,177
the papers from the internet
speculating about the cause.

764
00:51:45,178 --> 00:51:47,537
Nothing that can't be got
with hours of research.

765
00:51:47,538 --> 00:51:49,335
What about your bag?

766
00:51:49,378 --> 00:51:52,912
There's no startling new evidence.
It's all available.

767
00:51:52,938 --> 00:51:56,497
- Cumulatively, it amounts to what?
- Well, that depends.

768
00:51:56,498 --> 00:51:59,537
How long the government
and pharmaceutical companies

769
00:51:59,538 --> 00:52:04,017
keep on saying the rise in neural deficit
in these soldiers is coincidental.

770
00:52:04,018 --> 00:52:06,137
Neil says it's due to
better reporting by GPs.

771
00:52:06,138 --> 00:52:08,017
He speaks on health as well now?

772
00:52:08,018 --> 00:52:11,297
The families of those damaged soldiers
aren't buying that, Mum.

773
00:52:11,298 --> 00:52:13,448
Time you got a properjob, Chas.

774
00:52:13,498 --> 00:52:15,853
I'll borrow a laptop.

775
00:52:15,898 --> 00:52:18,165
I'll do the work in half the time.

776
00:52:22,258 --> 00:52:26,217
Information freely available
on the internet

777
00:52:26,258 --> 00:52:31,173
is not information
free to commercial enterprise.

778
00:52:31,218 --> 00:52:35,769
Internet Raiders stole not one
of my published ideas, but 27.

779
00:52:35,818 --> 00:52:40,334
Three of these ideas, including
my online data recovery software,

780
00:52:40,378 --> 00:52:46,055
have made them a great deal
of money - over 200 million.

781
00:52:46,098 --> 00:52:51,809
With a modest 10$% royalty,
they would owe me 20 million.

782
00:52:51,858 --> 00:52:54,577
I do intend to recover
what is rightfully mine,

783
00:52:54,578 --> 00:52:59,777
and to this end,
I will be calling 127 witnesses...

784
00:52:59,818 --> 00:53:01,497
Yes, I saw your list, Mr McCartney.

785
00:53:01,498 --> 00:53:05,730
It presages an even longer event
than the McLibel trial.

786
00:53:05,778 --> 00:53:10,645
The legal costs alone to the claimants
in that case were over? 10,000,000.

787
00:53:10,658 --> 00:53:14,017
Internet Raiders Incorporated
must be quaking at the prospect.

788
00:53:14,018 --> 00:53:17,010
Internet Raiders are a cash-rich company

789
00:53:17,058 --> 00:53:20,892
which is being financially prejudiced
by this action.

790
00:53:20,938 --> 00:53:25,011
We would like to either see
this spurious claim struck out

791
00:53:25,058 --> 00:53:30,815
or at least set a reasonable time-frame
of, say, one week?

792
00:53:30,858 --> 00:53:32,416
I doubt if Mr McCartney

793
00:53:32,458 --> 00:53:35,617
could read the qualifications
of his witnesses in that time.

794
00:53:35,618 --> 00:53:38,017
Well, then we respectfully request
your lordship

795
00:53:38,018 --> 00:53:42,330
utilises provision of
the Civil Procedure, Rule 35.1,

796
00:53:42,378 --> 00:53:46,337
and limits experts to... one per side.

797
00:53:46,378 --> 00:53:51,293
Mr McCartney has listed in detail
what ground his experts will cover.

798
00:53:51,338 --> 00:53:53,657
No, what I am going to do now
is adjourn

799
00:53:53,658 --> 00:53:56,137
so that each of the parties
can speak to the other.

800
00:53:56,138 --> 00:53:59,097
Who knows, you might even
reach a settlement. Talk.

801
00:53:59,098 --> 00:54:00,937
I don't mind if it takes a week.

802
00:54:00,938 --> 00:54:02,530
All rise.

803
00:54:09,578 --> 00:54:12,172
I feel like I've escaped prison.

804
00:54:12,218 --> 00:54:14,857
Mr McCartney wants is day in court, Judge.

805
00:54:14,858 --> 00:54:17,133
He's had four already!

806
00:54:17,178 --> 00:54:20,090
Sir Monty Everard sends his regrets.

807
00:54:20,138 --> 00:54:22,891
He's another who can't sit with you.

808
00:54:22,938 --> 00:54:26,248
Then I'll formally
open proceedings on my own.

809
00:54:26,298 --> 00:54:27,936
Warn the other parties.

810
00:54:27,978 --> 00:54:30,045
Do you think you should, Judge?

811
00:54:33,578 --> 00:54:36,251
I'm not sure we can stop Deed.

812
00:54:36,298 --> 00:54:38,697
It's not lawful, him sitting alone, is it?

813
00:54:38,698 --> 00:54:39,926
Probably not.

814
00:54:39,978 --> 00:54:42,697
Then if he does, it'll be struck down.

815
00:54:42,738 --> 00:54:45,650
Nothing encourages me to think I'd win.

816
00:54:45,698 --> 00:54:49,032
I don't want to spend a night in jail
for contempt.

817
00:54:52,578 --> 00:54:56,457
Is it contempt
if more asbestos were found?

818
00:54:58,178 --> 00:55:01,697
- More asbestos?
- That's what Brian Harrison told me.

819
00:55:01,698 --> 00:55:04,007
I want to see for myself.

820
00:55:10,258 --> 00:55:13,697
This court was perfectly usable
two hours ago, Mr Harrison.

821
00:55:13,698 --> 00:55:15,654
I know, Sir John. Asbestos.

822
00:55:15,698 --> 00:55:17,497
- Show it to me.
- I daren't, Sir John.

823
00:55:17,498 --> 00:55:20,177
Like some wild dangerous beast,
is it, ready to spring out?

824
00:55:20,178 --> 00:55:21,178
Sir John?

825
00:55:21,218 --> 00:55:22,817
If there was so much asbestos,

826
00:55:22,818 --> 00:55:25,777
half their lordships would be down
with mesothelioma.

827
00:55:25,778 --> 00:55:27,537
I can't breach ealth
and safety regulations.

828
00:55:27,538 --> 00:55:30,457
Find me an alternative court
or we'll hear it in the corridor.

829
00:55:30,458 --> 00:55:31,937
There isn't a court!

830
00:55:31,978 --> 00:55:35,578
You've got 30 minutes
or we'll hear it in the corridor.

831
00:55:37,138 --> 00:55:39,897
You ought to be ashamed of yourself,
Brian.

832
00:55:39,898 --> 00:55:41,937
I'm only following orders, Rita.

833
00:55:41,938 --> 00:55:44,452
That's even more shameful.

834
00:55:45,538 --> 00:55:49,577
My lord, I urge you not to hear
this case in the corridor.

835
00:55:49,618 --> 00:55:54,177
The court is anywhere a High Court judge
decides to preside over a lawful trial.

836
00:55:54,178 --> 00:55:57,257
Ajudge sitting alone
in the Divisional Court of the High Court

837
00:55:57,258 --> 00:55:59,567
is not lawful, my lord.

838
00:55:59,618 --> 00:56:02,937
Yes, I am aware of Section 66
of the Supreme Court Act, Ian.

839
00:56:02,938 --> 00:56:06,977
My lord, if you were to
ear this case in the corridor,

840
00:56:07,018 --> 00:56:11,285
it would look like transitional justice
in an emerging democracy.

841
00:56:16,138 --> 00:56:17,935
Yes. You're right.

842
00:56:17,978 --> 00:56:20,208
It should be heard in a court.

843
00:56:24,258 --> 00:56:26,697
And then this point is further developed

844
00:56:26,698 --> 00:56:31,032
in the respondent's skeleton argument,
my lord, at paragraph nine.

845
00:56:33,378 --> 00:56:36,377
Your lordship will see
that the case cited there

846
00:56:36,378 --> 00:56:38,737
is Charles and the Queen,
the Privy Council...

847
00:56:38,738 --> 00:56:43,057
Sir Joseph, Mr Justice Deed would like
to see you in his chambers urgently.

848
00:56:43,058 --> 00:56:48,690
Yes, well, this is, uh...
this is all very interesting,

849
00:56:48,738 --> 00:56:53,687
but I'll need to study
these papers in detail.

850
00:56:53,738 --> 00:56:55,217
All rise.

851
00:56:57,338 --> 00:57:00,297
Does this case have anything
to do with Charlie's mugging?

852
00:57:00,298 --> 00:57:02,016
Yes, I believe so.

853
00:57:02,058 --> 00:57:04,413
Why isn't Haughton doing something?

854
00:57:04,458 --> 00:57:09,525
The Home Secretary's too busy protecting
the interests of the drug companies.

855
00:57:11,818 --> 00:57:16,494
Joe, will you sit with me?

856
00:57:18,858 --> 00:57:22,977
You can use my court, but I don't think
I can have anything to do with it.

857
00:57:22,978 --> 00:57:26,857
You cannot lawfully sit on your own.

858
00:57:26,898 --> 00:57:30,777
It undermines the rule of law
and brings the bench into disrepute.

859
00:57:30,778 --> 00:57:35,613
Regardless, I'm going to take
a bold step forjustice and sit alone.

860
00:57:35,658 --> 00:57:39,537
If you won't join me,
any ruling I make will be invalid

861
00:57:39,578 --> 00:57:41,808
and I shall have been defeated.

862
00:57:41,858 --> 00:57:44,895
- But then so too will justice.
- John!

863
00:57:44,938 --> 00:57:47,327
John, don't be such a bloody fool!

